column1:
We are using 5.3.3.Final.  Same issue happens on 5.3.2.Final, and 5.3.1.Final runs fine.","[""This is a tough one to debug because as soon as our apps start, jgroups uses 100% of the CPU on all of the containers, and the DB becomes unresponsive.  Once I get the DB back online, I'm going to attempt to temporarily replace the JDBC_PING with FILE_PING to see if things work and at least narrow down where the problem is.
BTW...
I do have all the Jgroups source checked out, so if you have any idea based on the exception, where you might set a breakpoint and what to look for I'd be happy to check."", ""Here's what I know so far. When I looked at the DB logs what I can see is that hundreds of connections were made by jgroups all executing the same query: {{SELECT PING_DATA, OWN_ADDR, CLUSTER_NAME
FROM JGROUPSPING
WHERE CLUSTER_NAME=?. }}
{code}
Over 400+ connections were blocked because the database escalated the query to an exclusive row lock.  Why, I don't know yet.  My theory is there is some loop in the code somewhere, and it just starts looping, running that query repeatedly at such a high rate that the database decides to escalate it to a row lock to conserve resources.  But that's just a guess at this point.
{code}"", ""Moving from JDBC_PING to FILE_PING things are working again. By that I mean the environment doesn't crater. We are still getting the thousands of these exceptions...
java.io.EOFException at org.jgroups.util.ByteArrayDataInputStream.readByte(ByteArrayDataInputStream.java:159) at org.jgroups.util.Bits.makeInt(Bits.java:170) at org.jgroups.util.Bits.readIntCompressed(Bits.java:155) at org.jgroups.util.FlagsUUID.readFrom(FlagsUUID.java:73) at org.jgroups.util.ExtendedUUID.readFrom(ExtendedUUID.java:167) at org.jgroups.protocols.relay.SiteUUID.readFrom(SiteUUID.java:102) at org.jgroups.util.Util.readAddress(Util.java:1731) at org.jgroups.util.Util.readAddresses(Util.java:1843) at org.jgroups.protocols.PingData.readFrom(PingData.java:148) at org.jgroups.protocols.Discovery.deserialize(Discovery.java:633) at org.jgroups.protocols.Discovery.readPingData(Discovery.java:665) at org.jgroups.protocols.Discovery.handle(Discovery.java:320) at org.jgroups.protocols.Discovery.up(Discovery.java:299) at org.jgroups.protocols.TP.passMessageUp(TP.java:1184) at org.jgroups.util.SubmitToThreadPool$SingleMessageHandler.run(SubmitToThreadPool.java:107) at java.base/java.util.concurrent.ThreadPoolExecutor.runWorker(ThreadPoolExecutor.java:1144) at java.base/java.util.concurrent.ThreadPoolExecutor$Worker.run(ThreadPoolExecutor.java:642) at java.base/java.lang.Thread.run(Thread.java:1583)
So, some combination of 5.3.2 or 5.3.3, and JDBC_PING seems to be causing the stability issues, but the exception happens regardless."", ""Hmm. So 5.3.1 works, but 5.3.3 doesn't? Before doing a {code}
git bisect
{code}
, these issues could cause the problem:
- https://redhat.atlassian.net/browse/JGRP-2741
- https://redhat.atlassian.net/browse/JGRP-2756 (not likely)
In [inlineCard], I made a change to the discovery response, which also caused a regression (https://redhat.atlassian.net/browse/JGRP-2761) in KUBE_PING.
I'll investigate later this week if this change also caused a regression in JDBC_PING"", ""Do you have a thread dump at the time this happens? In a format I can read?"", ""I tested this with hsqldb, and looking at the code, and it seems that [inlineCard] is not the issue.
Can you reproduce this quickly? If so, to exclude object serialization as culprit, can you run either {code}
UPerf
{code}
 or {code}
MPerf
{code}
 on all nodes? If this works, then object serialization is the issue.
If not, then I suggest {code}
git bisect
{code}
 between {code}
jgroups-5.3.1.Final
{code}
 (good) and {code}
jgroups-5.3.2.Final
{code}
 (bad).
The core of the issue is that the received data is incorrect, and cannot be parsed."", ""Please also double-check that you're not running 5.3.3/5.3.2 and 5.3.1 instances in a mixed cluster"", ""{code}
initialize_sql=""""
{code}
 means that the table will not be created! So I assume that the {code}
JGROUPSPING
{code}
 table is pre-created by you?"", ""A couple of things to note. We've been using jgroups for probably over 5 years now, and we regularly upgrade every couple of months to stay current.  Our underlying code hasn't changed in years.  We use Jenkins to do nightly builds and automated deployments. Yesterday, before deploying 5.3.3, I manually wiped all the wars while troubleshooting and redeployed from Jenkins to ensure all the wars had the latest build.  A search for the jgroups jars show they are all using 5.3.3 across all the servers show 5.3.3 jars only.   We set initialize_sql="""" on purpose because the table is pre-created.
Since we were still having issues with FILE_PING, performance was not good at all, slow startups, high CPU, and hot deployments hanging.  I assumed whatever was affecting JDBC_PING performance may have also been impacting FILE_PING. With JDBC_PING and FILE_PING we have remove_all_data_on_view_change=""true"", which we had to enable because we were having issues because anytime a server would crash or someone would kill and restart a server it would abandon rows in the JGROUPSPING table and the abandoned rows would cause the entire environment to slow to a crawl.  Unfortunately, this setting also seems to cause quite a bit of thrashing of both FILE_PING and JDBC_PING because as the environment is coming online, it's constantly wiping out all the rows or files and rebuilding them as each node tries to join the cluster.
Anyways, to try and see if I can get to something stable on 5.3.3 I've moved from FILE_PING to TCPPING because our environment has multiple security apps (virus and malware scanners) that were scanning all the FILE_PING path and I was questioning if the virus scanner was creating file locks on the list files and I didn't feel like dealing with the security team to try and get them to add exclusions, that could take weeks to get approved. With FILE_PING we saw it take 15 to 20 minutes after the apps started for the cluster to form after startup.
So, at this point, I've moved back to your OOB example for TCPPING using tcp.xml verbatim; that way, we have no customizations.   One key thing to note is that in all our Tomcat clusters, we have parallel deployment in Tomcat by setting startStopThreads=0 to expedite deployment on startup. Setting startStopThreads=0 causes Tomcat to use separate threads, one per CPU, to start the applications in parallel. When we start or restart the environment, we will have 42 applications starting pretty close to the same time across 8 servers, all on the same subnet, and all joining the same JGROUPS cluster.
<Host name=""localhost"" appBase=""webapps""
unpackWARs=""true"" autoDeploy=""true""
startStopThreads=""0"" backgroundProcessorDelay=""5"">
On a side note, do you know of a setting we could change to stop all the apps from completing context initialization until the JGROUPS cluster is formed?  Is that the purpose of num_initial_members=""20""?
I will attempt to get you some thread dumps."", ""I looked at
https://redhat.atlassian.net/browse/JGRP-2741
https://redhat.atlassian.net/browse/JGRP-2756
https://redhat.atlassian.net/browse/JGRP-2761
We don't set {code}
return_entire_cache at all today, and looking at the code the default is false.  However, I was looking at enabling that today as well as PDC, but now that I know there are issues I will avoid it.
{code}

{code}
Initially I was suspecting revision 40a615e45c0550d5f91b4fd8c01a3b4950d9e5be.  The reason I say this is because the behavior we are seeing with JDBC_PING is like it is getting stuck in a loop querying the database and this is the only place I could see that happening.  Keep in mind that we have
{code}

remove_all_data_on_view_change=""true"".
{{}}
 {code} for(Map.Entry<Address,PhysicalAddress> entry: cache.entrySet()) {                            Address addr=entry.getKey();                            // JGRP-1492: only return our own address, and addresses in view.                            if(addr.equals(local_addr)

column3:
(view != null && view.containsMember(addr))) {                                PhysicalAddress physical_addr=entry.getValue();                                sendDiscoveryResponse(addr, physical_addr, NameCache.get(addr), msg.getSrc(), isCoord(addr));                                String name=NameCache.get(addr);                                rsps.add(new PingData(addr, is_server, name, physical_addr).coord(isCoord(addr)));                            } {code}"", ""Is it normal for resolved_hosts to return an error or should it list all the hosts its resolved?

[media]"", ""- bundler: {code}
no-bundler
{code}
?
- {code}
num_discovery_runs=1000
{code}
? That's going to cause a lot of discovery requests
- {code}
break_on_coord_rsp=false
{code}
?
Looking at this JIRA issue again, I see that most exceptions are caused by parsing of bytes returned by a DB read. I'd like to double-check that bytes returned are correct. Let's discuss this over a phone call, if you want: belaban at mailbox dot org.
Another test might be to set {code}
remove_all_data_on_view_change
{code}
 to false and re-enable it after startup, just to see if that's the root cause."", ""I'll reach out next week to set up a call. I'll get the developer that wrote all the jgroups code on the call as well.  We would be happy to help test things if it will help you out.   We actually have a unit test that we are going to see if we can use to reproduce the problem. If we can reproduce it, we will attach it to the ticket."", ""OK. If you have a reproducer, it would be great to use some DB that doesn't require registration or a trial version, e.g. hsqldb"", "", I've added you to a Slack channel on our end we can use if you want to have a huddle to ask any specific questions. Looks like we will have availability next Tuesday.  I figured a Slack channel might make it easier to share and communicate any questions more efficiently.  In the meantime, I'll see if I can repro the issue with a junit test with DB2 first, if so, then I'll try hsqldb."", ""TUE works for me. I'm on CEST (Switzerland) time.
Email works better for me than slack."", ""Any update on this?"", ""What's the status of this issue? The last comment was from almost a year ago, so I'll close this unless I hear from you soon..."", "": We moved away from using JDBC; it was causing too many issues."", ""Interesting; projects such as Keycloak moved to {code}
JDBC_PING2
{code}
 because it always works. Note that they already have a DB configured.
Can I close this issue then?""]"

