{noformat}","[""I finally found out where the information on the file name is lost. It is in {{deferred_spaces.add()}}:
{code:c++}
    /* The file name must be unique. Keep the one with the latest LSN. */
    auto d= defers.begin();

    while (d != defers.end())
    {
      if (d->second.file_name != defer.file_name)
        ++d;
// …
        /* Reset the old tablespace name in recovered spaces list */
        recv_spaces_t::iterator it{recv_spaces.find(d->first)};
        if (it != recv_spaces.end() &&
            it->second.name == d->second.file_name)
          it->second.name = """";
        defers.erase(d++);
{code}
This assumption will obviously be violated when preparing a backup where tables by the same name have been created, renamed, and dropped. The input that produced  [^data.tar.xz] involved several {{CREATE TABLE}} in one schema, {{RENAME TABLE}} to another schema, and {{DROP SCHEMA cool_down}}, in a loop."", "" [^fbackup.tar.xz] is a data set that will require a more extensive fix: renaming the expected file names when {{fil_name_process()}} will be invoked on {{FILE_RENAME}} and the file is not found."", "" [^data-fts.tar.xz] is one more data set that fails to recover some backed-up files correctly. At least one file (tablespace 15, {{test/t2.ibd}}, created during the backup) would incorrectly be recovered as containing only NUL bytes."", ""I [revised the logic

Please test your Qt functions in Valgrind to eliminate these kinds of memory leaks.","[""I could not find anything wrt QGenericUnixServices which might have fixed this probable leak, the other one is imo no leak which Qt has control of.
: If you have other leaks then please provide a minimal, compilable example for them."", ""The other one is not a leak at all. It's a false positive. Valgrind is complaining about the runtime allocating memory for the thread local storage. Glibc's NPTL caches thread stacks and heaps.
QGenericUnixServices isn't in QtCore, so reassigning back to XCB."", ""One more thing: it's not a leak if the memory was still in use at exit time and only shows as leak because the program exited. I will not lift a finger to fix those and I recommend other developers find more important things to do with their time."", ""It's hard to tell what other examples would be unique memory leaks because something as simple as this already causes a memory leak, which is needed for most Qt programs:
```
#include <QApplication>
int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
QApplication app(argc, argv);
return 0;
}
```
This gives a valgrind log very similar to the one I already posted.
So it seems like the example in my original post wasn't quite minimal, sorry about that."", ""Try valgrind 3.22, the report is very different with this 3.18 version."", ""Valgrind Memcheck: Different ways to lose your memory
https://developers.redhat.com/blog/2021/04/23/valgrind-memcheck-different-ways-to-lose-your-memory""]"

After 60 seconds have elapsed from the request being received, a remoting HEARTBEAT message is sent by the EJB client side. Then, the EJB server sends a CONNECTION_ALIVE_ACK as an ack to HEARTBEAT, but closes the TCP connection immediately afterward. When the EJB server writes the ack to HEARTBEAT, undertow write-timeout has already been exceeded, so the TCP connection is closed from the server side without waiting for the remote EJB processing to complete. As a result, no remote EJB response is returned to the client.","[""I can reproduce the issue and I can confirm the connection is closed due to write timeout. I don't think this is a bug though, I would say it's a consequence of the configuration.
1. After client opens a connection, some initial communication happens to initiate the connection, doing discovery, etc.
2. If the write timeout is set, the last data written by server in above communication is what sets the timeout counter (expireTime variable).
3. The EJB then waits 60 seconds, during which there is no communication (unless hearthbeat is triggered).
4. After 60 seconds, the EJB writes some data again.
5. The first packet of data from 4. is written and immediately after that the write timeout is checked. It causes the time out, because writing the last data (from point 4.) finished after more then 60 seconds after the timeout counter was set in point 2.
I think this is how it's designed to work. I think setting heartbeat interval to lower than write timeout is how it should be approached if there is bean that is slow to generate data and write timeout must be set.
I think modifying {code}
suspendWrites()
{code}
 to reset the timeout counter would not help in general, because if there is no communication {code}
suspendWrites()
{code}
 is not executed either."", ""I added the change to the same PR used for [inlineCard].
The change is to reset timeout counter in {code}
WriteTimeoutStreamSinkConduit#suspendWrites()
{code}
 as was suggested by , which should help in situations when multiple remoting calls are performed during single connection."", ""Verified with EAP 7.4.13.GA-CR1."", ""As all verified issues should change to closed status, closing them with the bulk update.""]"

