https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.linq.enumerable.take?view=net-9.0#system-linq-enumerable-take-1(system-collections-generic-ienumerable((-0))-system-int32)]"", ""Note that LINQ also has a TakeLast method, but it is not yet supported by the C# driver."", ""Thanks for the quick response [~robert@mongodb.com]. The new behavior makes sense in a vacuum, though worth noting such an (undocumented, as best I can tell) change in behavior almost broke some core functionality on our end. Unit tests for the win!

Is there any alternate way to achieve the same effect that the current c# driver supports (short of staying on 3.1.0)?"", ""You can simulate:

{code:java}
.Select(x => x.Widgets.TakeLast(n)
{code}

using:

{code:java}
.Select(x => x.Widgets.Skip(x.Widgets.Length - n)
{code}

"", ""Thanks [~robert@mongodb.com]! As of 3.2.0 that works as expected and meets our needs.

For anyone else that might be reading this, note that the suggested workaround will NOT work for 3.1.0 and below and will instead throw the following:

 
{code:java}
MongoDB.Driver.Linq.ExpressionNotSupportedException: Expression not supported: j.Widgets.Skip((j.Widgets.Count - 2)).
    at MongoDB.Driver.Linq.Linq3Implementation.Translators.ExpressionToAggregationExpressionTranslators.MethodCallExpressionToAggregationExpressionTranslator.Translate(TranslationContext context, MethodCallExpression expression)
...{code}
In short: up until 3.1.0 use `{{{}Take(-N){}}}`, and as of 3.2.0 use `{{{}Skip(COUNT - N){}}}`.

 "", ""Thanks for the additional comment about behavior prior 3.2.0.

One note: using a negative argument to `Take` is not documented and it only works accidentally, not by design (depending on what you think ""works"" means in this case)."", ""Makes sense. For context, I came across the ""accidental"" behavior [here

column1:
SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free /home/nico/workspace/qt6-dev/qtwayland/src/client/qwayland-tablet-unstable-v2.cpp:350 in QtWayland::zwp_tablet_tool_v2::handle_removed(void*, zwp_tablet_tool_v2*) {code}","[""Any idea if there are recent changes that might affect this?"", ""https://codereview.qt-project.org/c/qt/qtwayland/+/490896 is likely related"", ""So far I haven't reproduced it though.  Tried sway, labwc and kwin with the intuos 3 tablet and mostly dev branch, although at some point I think the manual test was using 6.8.0.  Tried the device_information manual test, and the tablet example."", ""But my qwayland-tablet-unstable-v2.cpp:350 looks like this
 {code} void zwp_tablet_tool_v2::zwp_tablet_tool_v2_removed()
    {
    }

    void zwp_tablet_tool_v2::handle_removed(
        void *data,
        struct ::zwp_tablet_tool_v2 *object)
    {
        Q_UNUSED(object);
        static_cast<zwp_tablet_tool_v2 *>(data)->zwp_tablet_tool_v2_removed(); // line 350
    } {code} OTOH there is an override
 {code} void QWaylandTabletToolV2::zwp_tablet_tool_v2_removed()
{
    destroy();
    m_tabletSeat->toolRemoved(this);
} {code} Maybe it gets called on a dangling pointer after something got deleted in the wrong order.
If the problem is that the tool got deleted too soon, I already tried to prevent that by keeping old ones around for a while in m_deadTools.  They are deleted only in QWaylandTabletSeatV2::zwp_tablet_seat_v2_tool_added() and ~QWaylandTabletSeatV2().  You could try commenting out those qDeleteAll() lines one at a time and find the culprit perhaps?"", ""> Maybe it gets called on a dangling pointer after something got deleted in the wrong order.
Problem was that tools and pad are now parented to the tablet. When unplugging and the tablet object is removed first the object would be deleted but not the wayland proxy.

Fixed by https://codereview.qt-project.org/c/qt/qtwayland/+/606088"", ""Seems like we just missed 6.8.1""]"

